## ABSTRACT
Compared with monometallic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) that are synthesized by reacting inorganic metal ions or clusters with bidentate or multidentate ligands via hydrothermal or solvothermal methods, the construction of heterogeneous frameworks like at least two kinds of metal sites in the individual nodes is proved to be an effective way to modulate their properties for advanced catalysis, especially for selective catalysis and multifunctional catalysis. However, it is still very challenging to precisely characterize their microstructures and reveal the relationship among the composition, structure, and their performances. Therefore, it is necessary to summarize the recent progress on bimetallic MOFs for thermal catalysis. First, we summarize the synthesis strategies and characterization methods of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives. Second, the application of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives as catalysts in thermal catalysis is discussed, and the relationship among the active components, structures, and their properties is elucidated. Third, the potential challenges and prospects of bimetallic MOF based nanocatalysts are proposed. This review will bring some insights into the design and preparation of bimetallic MOFs based nanocatalysts in the future.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, bimetallic metal nodes, synthesis methods, function synergy, heterogeneous catalysis
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers [1], which are self-assembled by metal ions or metal clusters with bidentate or multidentate ligands, often possess ultra-large surface area, tunable pore size, tunable chemical composition and surface functionality, and well-defined metal nodes [2–16]. Because of their unique properties, MOFs have become a hot research topic in the field of catalysis during the past twenty years [17–29]. Although much progress has been made in heterogeneous catalysis by MOFs, there are still some challenging issues in catalysis with the use of monometallic MOFs, such as unsatisfactory catalytic activity and selectivity, poor stability, and difficulty in achieving multifunctional catalysis [30–36].To address above questions, one of the possible ways is to fabricate the bimetallic MOFs for catalysis [37–39], because bimetallic MOFs have some intriguing properties for catalysis. On the one hand, the bimetallic MOFs have more diverse catalytic active sites and adsorption sites than monometallic MOFs [40,41], making them exhibit excellent performance in selective catalysis and multifunctional catalysis [42–44]. On the other hand, the organic ligands can be covalently bonded to the two kinds of metal sites in individual node, which is beneficial to improve thermal and chemical stability. In addition, bimetallic MOFs can be used as templates for the synthesis of a variety of derivatives, including carbon-metal composites and metal compounds, with controllable structure and composition [42,45–48]. To date, bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives have made some progress in the field of heterogeneous catalysis [8,42,49–53]. Nevertheless, the composition and structure of bimetallic MOFs are still unclear, and the characterization techniques are very limited. Moreover, it is also very difficult to illustrate the relationship between the structure and catalytic performance. Therefore, to better understand the current situation of bimetallic MOFs for heterogeneous catalysis, we summarize the synthesis strategies and characterization methods of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives, and discuss their catalytic performances. Finally, we propose the potential challenges and future development prospects of bimetallic MOFs based nanocatalysts.
