## 2 Synthesis of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives
To date, there are various methods developed for the synthesis of MOFs, including hydrothermal methods [17, 54–57], solvothermal methods [58–60], microwave heating methods [2, 61–64], electrochemical methods [59, 65, 66], sonochemical methods [67–69], and water reflux synthesis methods [70–72]. Some of these synthetic methods are also applicable to the synthesis of bimetallic MOFs. In principle, the synthesis methods
can be divided into one-pot synthesis method and post-synthetic modification method [40]. Different from the synthesis of monometallic MOFs, bimetallic MOFs need to strictly control the nucleation rate and growth kinetics due to the different reaction kinetics of the two kinds of metal ions or clusters. In addition, different derivatives can be obtained by pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs under different gas atmospheres and temperatures, for example, carbon-metal composites prepared by pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere, metal oxides synthesized by pyrolysis in air or oxygen, and the crystal form of bimetallic MOFs partially preserved by pyrolysis at low temperatures.
## 2.1 Synthesis method of bimetallic MOFs
## 2.1.1 One-pot synthesis method
The one-pot method is to directly mix two kinds of metal ions or clusters with bidentate or multidentate ligands in a specific solvent system via solvothermal method, ultrasonic method, or microwave method [53, 73]. One-pot synthesis is an entropy-driven process that can spontaneously transform disordered ligands into ordered molecular structures [53]. To avoid phase separation in bimetallic MOFs, it is necessary to strictly control the incorporation of metal ions, the solubility of metal ions, the reaction temperature, and the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of the reaction mixture. The synthesis methods will be introduced according to the types of bimetallic MOFs such as isovalent and heterovalent MOFs.
## 2.1.1.1 Synthesis of isovalent bimetallic MOFs
A variety of isovalent bimetallic MOFs have been successfully synthesized using the one-pot method. For example, Wang et al.
synthesized the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Mg}_{x}\mathrm{Cu}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) nanomaterials by using different ratios of magnesium nitrate hexahydrate and copper nitrate trihydrate as metal salts and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DHTA) as organic ligands via a one-pot method (Fig. 1(a)). According to inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis, the \(\mathrm{Mg/Cu}\) ratio was adjusted to \(\mathrm{Mg}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\), \(\mathrm{Mg}_{0.2}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.8}\), and \(\mathrm{Mg}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.83}\). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns showed that \(\mathrm{Mg}_{x}\mathrm{Cu}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) was consistent with the spectrum of \(\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}\) or \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF-74}\), indicating that replacing \(\mathrm{Mg}\) with \(\mathrm{Cu}\) did not affect the crystal structure of \(\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}\). However, different \(\mathrm{Mg/Cu}\) molar ratios have obvious effects on the morphology and structure of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{x}\mathrm{Cu}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\). With the increase of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) content, the morphology of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{x}\mathrm{Cu}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) changed from prismatic petals to needle-like petals, while the size was close to that of \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF-74}\), and the structure is denser than the corresponding monometallic MOF-74 (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c)) [74]. Similarly, Sun et al. synthesized the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Mg}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) nanocatalysts by using different ratios of zinc nitrate hexahydrate and magnesium nitrate tetrahydrate as metal salts and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid as organic ligands via a one-pot method. The powder XRD (PXRD) pattern showed that \(\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Mg}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) had a similar crystal structure to MOF-74. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images showed that the morphology of \(\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Mg}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOF-74}\) gradually changed from needle-like structure to rod-like structure with the increase of \(\mathrm{Zn}\) ratio (Figs. 1(d)–1(f)). Elemental mapping showed that \(\mathrm{Zn}\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}\) were uniformly distributed in the sample (Figs. 1(g)–1(i)). However, as for the position and coordination environment of \(\mathrm{Zn}\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}\), there is a lack of detailed characterization to explain [75].
In addition, Stock and co-workers synthesized a series of bimetallic Ce/Zr-UiO-66 and Ce/Zr-MOF-808 with different \( \mathrm{Ce}^{4+}/\mathrm{Zr}^{4+} \) molar ratios in a short period of time under mild reaction condition (Fig. 1(j)). The organic ligands 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right) \) and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\right) \) were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), respectively, and then, cerium(IV) ammonium nitrate, zirconium(IV) dinitrate oxide hydrate, and formic acid were added and stirred at \( 100\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 15{-}20\ \mathrm{\min} \) to obtain two bimetallic MOFs with high crystallinity. Variable temperature PXRD (VT-PXRD) and acid-alkali resistance experiments showed that bimetallic MOFs with \( \mathrm{Ce}\leq20 \) at. \(\%\) exhibited the enhanced thermal and chemical stability. This was attributed to that the presence of Ce in the framework was prone to generating defective sites and the low content of Ce in the Ce/Zr-UiO-66 resulted in low defects in the framework, where each cluster was missing at most one organic ligand, thus leading to the high stability [76].
In a word, two kinds of metal sites with the same valence state and bidentate or multidentate ligands can self-assemble into isovalent bimetallic MOFs via one-pot method. However, there are still some problems with one-pot method, such as the difficulty in controlling the distribution of the two kinds of metal sites in the individual node and the final ratio of the two kinds of metal sites.
## 2.1.1.2 Synthesis of heterovalent bimetallic MOFs
Two kinds of metal ions or clusters with different valence states are applied for synthesizing the heterovalent bimetallic MOFs. However, it is difficult to integrate different metal sites into the individual units due to the large difference in the hydrolysis and condensation rates of the two different metal ions. During the synthesis process, the solubility, ionic strength, and \( \mathrm{pH} \) value of the reaction system can be adjusted by changing the type and ratio of the co-solvent, and then the kinetic matching of different metal ions is modulated to synthesize the heterovalent bimetallic MOFs. For example, Feng et al. synthesized a series of bimetallic MOFs (CPM-200s) composed of trivalent metal ions \( \left(\mathrm{In}^{2+},\mathrm{Ga}^{2+},\mathrm{Fe}^{3+},\mathrm{V}^{3+},\right. \) and \( \mathrm{Sc}^{3+} \) ) and divalent metal ions \( \left(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+},\mathrm{Mn}^{2+},\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\right. \) and \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \) ) using a crystallization strategy (Fig. 2(a)). In/Mg-CPM-200 is synthesized by reacting \( \mathrm{InCl}_{3} \) and \( \mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2} \) with \( 3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime} \) - azobenzenetetracarboxylic acid \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{ABTC}\right) \) via controlling the co
solvent of \( N,N \) -dimethylacetamide (DMA) and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) with mass ratio of 5. By changing ratios of DMA and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) or using other cosolvents, the target bimetallic MOFs cannot be obtained. In addition, other metal ions with different valence states \( \left(\mathrm{Ga}^{3+},\mathrm{Fe}^{3+},\right. \) \( \left.\mathrm{V}^{3+},\mathrm{Sc}^{3+},\mathrm{Mn}^{2+},\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\right. \) and \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \) ) are also applicable for synthesizing a series of CPM-200s using the same strategy. The obtained CPM-200s showed high phase purity and good stability. \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption at 77 K showed the typical I-type behavior of activated CPM-200s [77]. Therefore, the type and ratio of co-solvents are crucial for the formation of heterovalent bimetallic MOFs.
Luque and co-workers dissolved copper(II) chloride hexahydrate, iron(III) chloride hexahydrate, and 2- aminoterephthalic acid in DMF and ethanol with volume ratio of 14 at \( 150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 24\,\mathrm{{h}} \) reaction, and finally obtained the spindle and octahedral heterovalent bimetallic \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) -BDC-FeCu (Figs. 2(b) and 2(c)). The asymmetric and symmetrical vibration peaks of the amino group at 3463 and \( 3333\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) in the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectrum indicated that the amino group was in a free state and did not coordinate with the metal ions in the \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) -BDC-FeCu. The disappearance of the –OH stretching vibration peak in 2-aminoterephthalic acid indicated the coordination of the metal ions to the –COOH group. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra showed the peaks at \( 530.2\ \mathrm{eV} \) for Fe–O, at \( 531.2\ \mathrm{eV} \) for C–O, and at \( 532.7\ \mathrm{eV} \) for \( \mathrm{O-C=O} \), as well as the peaks at \( 932.8\ \mathrm{eV} \) for \( \mathrm{Cu-O} \), indicating the formation of bimetallic MOFs. The XRD peaks at \( 35.7^{\circ} \) and \( 38.9^{\circ} \) related to the (110) and (002) phases, confirming that the successful incorporation of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) in the framework was achieved and the incorporation of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) did not affect the assembly process of \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) -BDC-Fe [78].
Besides, Zou and coworkers synthesized FeNi-MIL-53 with a one-pot method by dissolving nickel acetate tetrahydrate, iron sulfate, and terephthalic acid in water and DMA with volume ratio of 1 (Fig. 2(d)). The XRD pattern and FT-IR spectra showed that the FeNi-MIL-53 was consistent with the original Ni-MIL-53 and the simulated Ni-MIL-53, confirming that the introduction of Fe element did not destroy the crystal structure of Ni-MIL-53. The XPS spectra revealed the presence of C, O, Ni, and Fe elements in the FeNi-MIL-53, and the characteristic peaks at binding energies of 713.9 and \( 727.3\ \mathrm{eV} \) were ascribed to \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) and \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \),
respectively. SEM and TEM images showed the marigold-like morphology consisting of many layered structures of obvious ripples, indicating that the FeNi-MIL-53 was characteristic with a nanosheet structure (Figs. 2(e)–2(i)) [79].
Although two metal ions or clusters with different valence states can be used to prepare bimetallic MOFs, it is necessary to adjust the types of metal precursors and precisely control the type and ratio of co-solvents. In addition, it is still difficult to accurately control the distribution and ultimate ratio of the two kinds of metal sites in the framework during the synthesis process.
## 2.1.2 Post-synthetic modification method
The post-synthetic modification strategy is effective for synthesizing bimetallic MOFs, which can be used to synthesize unconventional bimetallic MOFs that are difficult or impossible to synthesize by one-pot method due to different reactivity, atomic radius, and electronegativity of metal ions or clusters [42, 53]. Postsynthetic modification mainly includes metal ion exchange and metal elimination-addition. The biggest challenge is to precisely control the ratio of the two kinds of metal sites in the bimetallic MOFs in a large range.
## 2.1.2.1 Metal ion exchange strategy
Metal ion exchange is one of the effective strategies for the synthesis of bimetallic MOFs. By using this method, the monometallic MOFs are usually added into the second metal ions solution with similar reactivity and electronegativity to achieve ion exchange with metal nodes in the framework. During the ion exchange process, the degree of exchange can be regulated by changing parameters such as concentration, time, and temperature. Kim et al. dissolved \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and
\(5,5^{\prime},10,10^{\prime},15,15^{\prime}\) -hexaethyltruxene-2,7,12-tricarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{hett}\right)\) in DMF solution to synthesize MOFs \((\mathrm{Cd}_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{3}[(\mathrm{Cd}_{4}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{hett})_{6}]\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) with cubic networks and channels. Then, it was soaked in an aqueous lead nitrate solution for \(2\,\,\mathrm{h},\) and \(98\%\) of the cadmium ions in the framework were replaced by lead ions. After two days, the cadmium ions were completely replaced by the lead ions (Fig. 3(a)). Optical microscopy, in-situ PXRD, and single crystal XRD (SXRD) confirmed that the crystal structure and morphology of the MOFs hardly changed during the metal ion exchange process, proving that this process was a single-crystal to single-crystal transition. When the metal nodes were replaced by lead ions and then were soaked in a cadmium nitrate solution, about \(50\%\) of the lead ions were replaced by cadmium ions after one day, and it took nearly three weeks for the complete exchange, indicating that the metal ion exchange process was a reversible process and takes a long time to complete [80].
Due to the similar arrangement of electrons between the two transition metal sites, most of the reported metal ion exchange processes occur between transition metals, while there are few reports on the exchange of alkaline earth metals. For the first time, \(Z\mathrm{n-MOF-74}\) synthesized from \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and DHTA could undergo alkaline earth metal exchange. In detail, \(Z\mathrm{n-MOF-74}\) was soaked in DMF solution of \(\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}\) for metal site exchange at temperature range from 25 to \(100\,^{\circ}\!C.\) After 7 days at \(75\,^{\circ}\!C,\) about \(33\%\) of the zinc ions in the framework were replaced by calcium ions. When the temperature dropped to \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) only \(20\%\) of the zinc ions in \(Z\mathrm{n-MOF-74}\) were substituted. With raising the exchange temperature up to \(100\,^{\circ}\!C,\) the exchange percentage of zinc ions reached \(68\%\) , but the pore size analysis showed that the exchange at high temperature caused the destruction of the pore
structure. The time-of-flight mass spectrometry (TOFMS) of Ca/Zn-MOF-74 (Figs. 3(b) and 3(c)) showed a new signal of calcium cation in the positive spectrum, and no signal of chloride anion in the negative ion spectrum. It can be deduced that calcium cation had the indeed replaced the zinc cation in Zn-MOF-74, rather than as a chloride salt in the channels of Zn-MOF-74 [81].
In a word, two kinds of metal ions with similar reactivity and electronegativity can be used to synthesize bimetallic MOFs through an ion-exchange strategy. However, there are some challenges during the synthesis process, such as accurately controlling the exchange amount and exchange position of the two kinds of metal sites.
## 2.1.2.2 Metal elimination–addition strategy
The orderly removal of some metal sites in monometallic MOFs to form vacancies, which are then repaired with different kinds of metal ions, is another effective strategy for the synthesis of bimetallic MOFs. Note that by using this way, new metal ions should have similar charge and coordination modes to the eliminated metal ions. Li and co-workers used a porous MOF \((\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{PyC})_{3})\) composed of \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{ON}_{12}\) units linked by 4-pyrazolecarboxylate (PyC) as templates to synthesize bimetallic MOFs using metal elimination-addition strategy (Fig. 4(a)). First, \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PyC}\) were heated in N,N-diethylformamide (DEF) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) by solvothermal method to obtain single crystal \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{PyC})_{3}\). Then, \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{PyC})_{3}\) was immersed in water for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) at room temperature, and half of the ligands and a quarter of the metal ions were removed to obtain \(\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{1}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{PyC})_{1.5}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3.5}(\mathrm{PyC})_{0.5}\) (□: vacancy). This process preserved the original single crystallinity and unit size, while the pore size increased. The formed metal vacancies can be repaired with other metal ions (e.g., \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{La}^{3+}\)), and ligand vacancies can be filled with new functionalized ligands (e.g., \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{-PyC}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-PyC}\)). The repair process can be done individually or in one-pot process [82].
In addition, T. Nguyen et al. developed a general demetallization-remetallation strategy to synthesize a series of
metallosalen-based MOFs with different coordinatively unsaturated metal sites. Porous and noncatenated \(\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}\)(salen)-based MOFs \((\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\) SO-MOFs) with \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) node were first synthesized using tetracarboxylic acid and dipyridyl Mn (salen) strut, and then soaked in a mixed solution of methanol and hydrogen peroxide for "demanganization" to obtain dSO-MOF (Fig. 4(b)). Inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) showed that the \(\mathrm{Zn/Mn}\) ratio increased from 2 to 20. Finally, dSO-MOF was added into a solution containing \(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\) salts \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r},\,\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o},\,\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n},\,\mathbf{N}\mathbf{b},\,\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u},\,\mathbf{a}\) nd \(\mathrm{Zn})\) under nitrogen atmosphere and shaken for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to obtain remetallated MSO-MOF [83].
In a word, bimetallic MOFs can be efficiently synthesized using the metal elimination-addition strategy. However, this synthetic method has some limitations, such as the limited amount of metal removal and the limited choice of metal sites for remediation.
## 2.2 Synthesis of bimetallic MOF derivatives
In recent years, the pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs to obtain derivatives such as carbon-metal composites, metal compounds, and metal oxide-carbon composites has attracted extensive attention. The synthesis of bimetallic MOFs derivatives is usually achieved by calcination of the parent MOFs at high temperature to lose the organic ligands. These derived materials have shown great potential in gas storage and separation, catalysis, sensing, energy storage, etc. [84–87].
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of carbon-metal composites
Calcining bimetallic MOFs at high temperatures under an inert atmosphere can prepare carbon-metal composites with various structures and compositions. The derived carbon composites by pyrolysis generally have the well-defined pore structure and high specific surface area, which is conducive to the free diffusion of substrates and further enhances the catalytic performance. Yan and co-workers prepared a series of bimetallic MOF-derived carbon supported NiCo catalysts (NiCo/C) with abundant oxygen vacancies by pyrolysis. First, bimetallic NiCo-BTCs with different
Ni/Co molar ratios of 0.5, 1, and 2 were synthesized by reacting nickel nitrate hexahydrate, cobalt nitrate hexahydrate, and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid via hydrothermal method. The obtained NiCo-BTCs were then heated up to \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in argon to prepare \(\mathrm{NiCo/C}\) with different ratios. The K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra showed that the valence state of Ni was zero-valent, and the valence state of Co was between \(C0^{0}\) and \(C0^{2+}\) (Figs. 5(a) and 5(b)). Extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy (EXAFS) spectra showed that the peak of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}/\mathrm{C}\) decreased to \(2.39\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\), indicating the formation of Ni-Co nanoalloys. The peak at \(3.34\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) corresponded to the Ni-O-Co species, indicating coordination between the \(\mathrm{Ni/Co}\) atoms and the oxygen groups in the carbon support. EXAFS curve fitting results showed that the first shell coordination numbers of Ni or \(\mathrm{Co/C}\), Ni-Co and Ni-C-Co in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}/\mathrm{C}\) were 1, 6, and 4 (Figs. 5(c) and 5(d)), respectively [88].
Shin et al. developed the two-step annealing of \(\mathrm{Co/Zn-MOF-5}\) to synthesize a Co-dispersed hierarchical porous carbon composite \((\mathrm{Co@C})\). First, bimetallic \(\mathrm{Co/Zn-MOF-5}\) was synthesized using cobalt nitrate hexahydrate and zinc nitrate hexahydrate as metal precursors and terephthalic acid as an organic ligand by solvothermal method. Then, the \(\mathrm{Co/Zn-MOF-5}\) was subjected to a two-step heat treatment process as follows: first calcined at \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in a nitrogen atmosphere to generate \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), and then annealed at \(1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to remove the generated \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), thereby forming pores on the generated carbon material. The formed Co nanoparticles uniformly distributed on the porous carbon support during the annealing process. The diffraction peaks of Co (111) and (200) crystals in \(\mathrm{Co@C}\) samples proved the formation of Co nanoparticles, while the disappearance of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) diffraction peaks indicated the complete removal of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) during high-temperature calcination. TEM and TEM-energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images showed that Co nanoparticles with a diameter of about \(20\,\mathrm{nm}\) were uniformly distributed in the porous carbon support (Figs. 5(e)–5(g)). \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption–desorption curves showed a type IV isotherm with a
hysteresis loop for \(\mathrm{Co@C}\), indicative of mesoporous properties and a wider pore size distribution [89].
In a word, carbon-metal composites can be prepared by high-temperature pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs under inert atmosphere. The obtained carbon-metal materials not only have high specific surface area and well-defined pore structure, but also facilitate the dispersion of metal species. In addition, the expected structure can be obtained by adjusting the calcination temperature. However, this requires the accurate control of temperature changes.
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of metal compounds
Bimetallic oxide derivatives can be obtained by calcination of bimetallic MOFs in air. Using bimetallic MOFs as sacrificial templates, the structure, composition, and morphology of the obtained bimetallic derivatives can be regulated through a specific heat treatment process. Tan et al. prepared hierarchical microflower \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) assembled by pyrolyzing \(\mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIF-MFs}\) (MFs: microflowers, ZIF: zeolitic imidazolate framework). First, the \(\mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIF-MFs}\) were synthesized using zinc nitrate hexahydrate and cobalt nitrate hexahydrate as metal salts and 2-methylimidazole as an organic ligand via a one-pot hydrothermal method (Fig. 5(h)). Then, the obtained \(\mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIF-MFs}\) were heated to 405 and \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air for \(55\,\mathrm{min}\) and \(2\,\mathrm{h}\), respectively, and \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-MFs and \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-BMFs (BMFs: broken microflowers) were obtained (Figs. 5(i) and 5(j)). The microflower structure was characteristic of higher specific surface area and pore volume, while the broken microflower structure featured a lower specific surface area due to the higher annealing temperature [90].
In addition, sequential pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs in inert and air atmospheres can increase the specific surface area of bimetallic oxides and preserve the original structure of MOFs to a certain extent. Zhou and co-workers synthesized the bimetallic zeolite imidazolate framework \(\mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIFs}\) by co-precipitation using \(\mathrm{Zn}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}\) as metal nodes and 2-methylimidazole as ligand (Fig. 5(k)). The obtained \(\mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIFs}\) were first heated in nitrogen at \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and then continued to be heated in air at the same
temperature, and finally \( \mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) hollow polyhedra was obtained. No other peaks were observed in the XRD pattern, indicating that the \( \mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIFs} \) were completely transformed into bimetal oxide. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) analysis showed that the specific surface area of \( \mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) is \( 65.58\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \), much higher than \( 41.83\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \) for yolk–shell \( \mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) microspheres by pyrolysis of \( \mathrm{ZnCo} \)-glycolate microspheres [92]. Field-emission SEM (FESEM) and TEM images showed that \( \mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) retained the size and rhombic dodecahedral shape of the \( \mathrm{Zn/Co-ZIF} \) precursor after annealing (Fig. 5(l)–5(o)). The two-step annealing method preserved the morphology of the ZIFs, mainly because the carbon produced by the first heat treatment in nitrogen acted as a temporal buffer, preventing further shrinkage of the ZIFs framework during treatment in air [91].
In a word, bimetallic oxides can be obtained by calcining bimetallic MOFs at high temperatures in air. The two-step annealing method can retain the original structure of bimetallic MOFs and it is beneficial to improve the dispersion of bimetallic oxides, which requires accurate control of the annealing temperature during the calcination process.
## 2.2.3 Synthesis of metal oxide-carbon composites
Metal oxide-carbon composites can be obtained by calcining bimetallic MOFs-carbon materials. Kim and co-workers used porous carbon nanofibers (PCNFs) as templates to vertically grow Ni/Fe-MIL-101 on the surface for obtaining Ni/Fe-MIL-101@PCNFs. Then, Ni/Fe-MIL-101@PCNFs were calcined at
different temperatures of 200, 400, and \( 600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) to obtain different Ni-Fe-O/NPC@PCNFs (NPC: nanoporous carbon) composites (Figs. 6(a)–6(c)). The XRD pattern showed that with the increase in calcination temperature, the crystallinity gradually decreased, and more defects on the surface were generated [93]. Besides, Chen et al. synthesized the novel MOF-derived nanocomposites with reduced graphene oxide \( \left(\mathrm{rGO}\right) \) supported mesoporous \( \mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \), denoted as \( \mathrm{rGO@ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \). First, GO was mixed with the precursors for the synthesis of \( \mathrm{ZnCo-ZIF} \), and then, \( \mathrm{rGO@ZnCo-ZIF} \) was synthesized after microwave heating. Due to the thermal reduction property of the solvent, GO would be reduced to rGO during heating. Finally, the obtained \( \mathrm{rGO@ZnCo-ZIF} \) was calcined in air at different temperatures to prepare the \( \mathrm{rGO@ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) composite. The XRD pattern showed that the peak of ZIFs disappeared after calcination, and the diffraction peak corresponding to cubic \( \mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) appeared (Fig. 6(d)). TEM images showed that \( \mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) crystals were uniformly distributed on the rGO nanosheets (Fig. 6(e)) [94].
Taking advantage of the difference in thermal stability of metal–O coordination bonds, calcination at low temperatures can preserve the partial structure of bimetallic MOFs. Amal et al. developed a controllable heat treatment method to directly encapsulate Ni nanoparticles in Mg-MOF-74 to form a \( \mathrm{Ni@Mg-MOF-74} \) structure. First, NiMg-MOF-74 with different ratios was synthesized using nickel acetate tetrahydrate and magnesium nitrate hexahydrate as metal salts and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid as organic ligands. Using the difference in thermal stability of
Ni–O and \(\mathrm{Mg-O}\) coordination bonds in NiMg-MOF-74, part of the framework of Mg-MOF-74 was preserved after heat treatment at \(350\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . While Ni–O bond was transformed into Ni nanoparticles with a diameter of less than \(5~\mathrm{nm}\) and uniformly distributed in the Mg-MOF-74 framework (Fig. 6(f)). XRD spectrum showed a relatively weak diffraction peak for \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{Mg}-\) MOF-74, indicating the presence of MOF-74 framework in the sample. FT-IR spectra indicated that the framework of \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{Mg}-\) MOF-74 was mainly composed of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) node and 2,5- dihydroxyterephthalic acid. A pair of new spin-orbit splitting peaks were observed at the binding energy of 853.2 and \(870.7~\mathrm{eV}\) in the XPS spectrum, indicating the formation of \(\mathrm{Ni^{0}}\) . In addition, \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{Mg}-\mathrm{MOF}-74\) revealed a shift to lower edge energy, consistent with the XPS results of partial reduction of \(\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni^{0}}\) [95].
In summary, different methods can be used to prepare bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives with different structures and functions (Table 1). It is worth noting that different synthetic strategies determine the morphology and structure of catalysts, which may lead to significant differences in catalytic performance.
## 3 Application of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives in thermal catalysis
Although MOFs have exhibited good catalytic performances in some cases, the singleness of active sites makes it difficult to achieve selective and multifunctional catalysis. In contrast, bimetallic MOFs can catalyze more challenging reactions due to their two different active sites, diverse coordination modes, and tunable compositions and structures. In addition, bimetallic MOFs can be used to synthesize a variety of derived functional materials, including carbon-metal composites and metal compounds, which have a wide range of applications in thermal catalysis due to their unique physiochemical properties. In this section, we present the recent progress in the application of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives in the field of thermal catalysis.
## 3.1 Application of bimetallic MOFs in thermal catalysis
Bimetallic MOFs have shown the promising catalytic performances in a variety of reactions, including oxidation reactions and tandem reactions, etc.
## 3.1.1 Oxidation reactions
Bimetallic MOFs play an important role in the selective oxidation reactions. Wang et al. synthesized a two-dimensional FeCo-BDC using triethylamine as a morphology control agent for the oxidation of rhodamine B (RhB). In the presence of peroxymonosulfate (PMS), the FeCo-BDC nanosheets achieved a degradation efficiency of \(99.1\%\) within \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and a total organic carbon (TOC) removal rate of \(44.9\%\) , indicating that FeCo-BDC could effectively activate PMS. In contrast, the RhB degradation efficiency decreased to \(93.4\%\) for Co-BDC and \(4.2\%\) for Fe-BDC, and corresponding TOC removal rate also decreased to \(28.2\%\) and \(0.1\%\) (Figs. 7(a) and 7(b)). The high catalytic performance of FeCoBDC was mainly due to the activation of PMS by \(\mathrm{Co^{2+}/F e^{2+}}\) to generate \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{-}\) , which further reacted with water molecules to generate \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) . At the same time, PMS and dissolved oxygen in water produced \(\mathrm{O}_{2}{}^{-}\) , and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}{}^{-}\) further reacted with \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) to transform into \(\mathrm{^{\prime}O}_{2}\) . Finally, organic molecules were decomposed by active oxygen \(\mathrm{O}_{2}{}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{^{\prime}O}_{2}\) . Besides, the electron transfer between bimetal sites effectively accelerated the redox process of \(\mathrm{Co^{3+}/C o^{2+}}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/F e^{2+}}\) , thereby promoting the formation of reactive oxygen species. In addition, FeCo-BDC nanosheets also showed excellent performance (degradation efficiency \(>95\%\) ) for the selective oxidation of Congo red, RhB, and methyl orange in dye wastewater [96].
Similarly, Tang et al. synthesized bimetallic \(\mathrm{Cu}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}\) -MOF-74 via a one-pot method for the oxidation of organic pollutant methylene blue (MB). In the presence of PMS, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}\) -MOF-74 achieved almost complete MB degradation within \(30~\mathrm{\min}\) . Whereas in the absence of catalyst, the removal rate of MB by PMS was only \(30\%\) , indicating that \(\mathrm{Cu}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{1}\) -MOF-74 effectively
activated PMS. As a comparison, the MB removal rate of and under the same conditions were \(33.1\%\) for \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF-74}\) and \(74.0\%\) for Co-MOF-74. The high catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Cu_{1}C o_{1}}\)-MOF-74 was mainly attributable to the effective activation of PMS by \(\mathrm{Cu/Co}\) to generate \(\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{-}\)and -OH, and further generated \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to promote the degradation of MB. In addition, the MB removal rate of \(\mathrm{Cu_{1}C o_{1}}\)-MOF-74 gradually decreased to \(76.4\%\) in five consecutive stability tests, which was mainly due to the leaching of metal ions [100].
Styrene oxidation is widely used in the production of pharmaceuticals and fine chemicals, so it is of great importance to develop the robust catalysts for achieving the green oxidation of styrene. Huang et al. reported co-doping Co in Cu-BTC to prepare a series of \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-BTC by using hydrothermal method for selective oxidation of styrene in the presence of t-butylhydroperoxide (TBHP) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). Without a catalyst, the oxidation of styrene did not occur. The Cu-BTC catalyst exhibited the \(92.5\%\) conversion rate of styrene, but the selectivity to styrene oxide was only \(33.9\%\). When Co-BTC was used as the catalyst, the conversion rate was \(72.4\%\), while a high selectivity to styrene oxide of \(77.7\%\) was obtained. Under the identical conditions, the conversion of \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-BTC remained around \(98\%\), while the selectivity of styrene oxide increased with the increase of Co content in \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-BTC. Specifically, the selectivity increased from \(51.1\%\) over \(\mathrm{Cu_{0.75}C o_{0.25}}\)-BTC to \(83.0\%\) over \(\mathrm{Cu_{0.25}C o_{0.75}}\)-BTC, indicating a significant synergistic effect of the \(\mathrm{Cu/Co}\) site in \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-BTC with respect to Cu-BTC and Co-BTC. The incorporation of Co changed the electron density around Cu and improved the catalytic activity and selectivity. Besides, the \(\mathrm{Cu_{0.25}C o_{0.75}}\)-BTC catalyst exhibited the satisfactory recyclability (Fig. 7(c)) [97].
Fan et al. synthesized \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-MOF-74 catalysts with different \(\mathrm{Cu/Co}\) ratios for aerobic styrene oxidation under solvent-free and mild reaction conditions. When the oxidation of styrene was
carried out using \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) as the oxidant under \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\), Cu-MOF-74 had a rather low catalytic activity of \(0.6\%\), but an absolute selectivity of benzaldehyde, and no oxidized styrene was produced. Co-MOF-74 had a relatively high catalytic activity of \(47.3\%\), but a low selectivity of \(35\%\) for benzaldehyde, and the selectivity to styrene oxide was \(17\%\). By increasing the Co sites in \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-MOF-74, the conversion of styrene was increased from \(10.2\%\) of \(\mathrm{Cu_{0}C o_{1}}\)-MOF-74 to \(30.4\%\) of \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}C o_{7}}\)-MOF-74, and the selectivity of styrene oxidation was increased from \(15\%\) to \(35\%\), while the selectivity of benzaldehyde decreased from \(75\%\) to \(43\%\). It was interesting to note that the activity and the selectivity to styrene oxide of \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-MOF-74 catalyst were higher than the physical mixture of Cu-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 with almost the same amount of Cu and Co. Thus, the higher activity of \(\mathrm{Cu_{x}C o_{y}}\)-MOF-74 was ascribed to the synergistic effect of Cu and Co sites in the same framework, where Co promoted the activation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), while the possible valence cycle of \(\mathrm{Co^{2+}}\) and \(\mathrm{Co^{3+}}\) was favorable for producing styrene oxide. In addition, the incorporation of Cu effectively inhibited the polymerization of styrene. During a six-cycle test, the conversion rate and selectivity changed slightly over \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}C o_{7}}\)-MOF-74, implying that the catalyst had good cycling stability (Fig. 7(d)) [98].
Different from above, Li et al. introduced \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) single sites into bimetallic CuCo-MOF-74 by partially reducing \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) to \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) ions during a solvothermal reaction and used for aerobic oxidation reaction of styrene under air atmosphere (Figs. 7(e) and 7(f)). When Cu-MOF-74 was used as the catalyst, the conversion of styrene and the selectivity of styrene oxide were \(60.2\%\) and \(55.4\%\), respectively. However, Co-MOF-74 showed a conversion rate of \(92.3\%\) to styrene and a selectivity to styrene oxide of \(69.6\%\). When Cu sites were introduced into Co-MOF-74, the selectivity of styrene oxide was further enhanced. Among different CuCo-MOF-74 samples, \(\mathrm{Cu_{1/2}C o_{1/2}}\)-MOF-74 showed a selectivity of \(77.6\%\) for styrene oxide at almost complete conversion of styrene.
As a comparison, when the physical mixture of Cu-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 was used as the catalyst, the conversion of styrene was \(87.0\%\), and the selectivity of styrene oxide was only \(74.5\%\). Such excellent performance mainly originated from the partial density of state (pDOS) of the Co-3d orbital closer to the Fermi level by modulating with \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) sites for promoting the activation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) compared with dual-sites \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\), \(\mathrm{Co(II)}\), \(\mathrm{Co(II)}\), and \(\mathrm{Co(II)}\), thus contributing to the epoxidation of the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) bond. The catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{1/2}\mathrm{Co}_{1/2}\)-MOF-74 displayed almost no decay during nine consecutive cycle tests, indicating the good stability. In addition, when styrene derivatives including 4-methylstyrene, 2-methylstyrene, 4-fluorostyrene, 3-chlorostyrene, and 4-chlorostyrene were used as substrates, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{1/2}\mathrm{Co}_{1/2}\)-MOF-74 also exhibited the excellent activity and selectivity of epoxide [99].
In a word, the introduction of additional active metal centers in the original MOFs is an effective way to improve their catalytic performance due to the possible synergistic effect of the two kinds of active metal sites during the catalytic process.
## 3.1.2 Tandem reactions
Bimetallic MOFs show great potential for tandem catalysis and multifunctional synergistic catalysis due to the bimetallic active sites, various coordination modes, and tunable compositions and structures that can realize multi-step reactions with high activity and excellent selectivity, and avoid time-consuming and tedious intermediate purification/separation processes.
Fan et al. prepared \(\mathrm{Al/Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) as acid-base bifunctional catalysts for Deacetalization-Knoevenagel condensation cascade reaction at \(90\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and atmospheric pressure (Figs. 8(a) and 8(b)). The yield was \(90\%\) over Al-MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)
and \(70\%\) over \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) within \(90\ \mathrm{\min}\), while the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Al/Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) showed the enhanced yield close to \(100\%\) after \(90\ \mathrm{min}\) reaction. In particular, the \(\mathrm{Al/Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) with \(\mathrm{Al/Fe}\) ratio of 15 showed the yield of nearly \(35\%\) after only \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) reaction. As a comparison, physically mixing of Al-MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) showed the significantly reduced yield of \(89.7\%\), indicative of the presence of synergistic effect between bimetallic sites in the framework for the enhanced performance. During the reaction, the unsaturated \(\mathrm{Al}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}\) served as acidic sites to adsorb benzaldehyde dimethyl acetal, and further catalyze the hydrolysis reaction to produce benzaldehyde. At the same time, \(\mathrm{Al}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}\) ions synergistically adsorbed benzaldehyde through the coordination of oxygen in metal clusters, activated carbonyl dehydrogenation and made it partially negatively charged, so that it was easily attacked by malononitrile nucleophiles to form the final product. In addition, the \(\mathrm{Al/Fe-MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) with \(\mathrm{Al/Fe}\) ratio of 15 showed the strong stability, excellent recyclability, and durable catalytic activity (Fig. 8(c)) [101].
Clarke et al. reported a mixed-metal \(\mathrm{Sc/Fe-MIL-100}\) as a bifunctional Lewis acid catalyst for Friedel–Crafts addition plus subsequent oxidation reactions. As for one-pot tandem reaction of 2-methylindole with trifluoroacetaldehyde ethyl hemiacetal and tert-butyl hydroperoxide at room temperature, the conversion rate was \(10\%\) over \(\mathrm{Sc-MIL-100}\) and \(60\%\) over \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-100}\) after \(6\ \mathrm{h}\) reaction. However, \(\mathrm{Sc/Fe-MIL-100}\) catalyst achieved a conversion rate of \(96\%\) under the identical condition (Fig. 8(d)). Although a simple physical mixture of \(\mathrm{Sc-MIL-100}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-100}\) with the same \(\mathrm{Sc/Fe}\) ratio can also catalyze the cascade reaction, the conversion rate was only \(78\%\). Note that \(\mathrm{Sc}^{3+}\) node acted as a
highly active Lewis acid site for the addition reaction, while \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) node catalyzed the oxidation reaction. Both of them promoted the tandem reaction together. In addition, when 2-methylindole was replaced by other indole derivatives such as indole, N-methylindole, pyrrole, and dimethoxybenzene, Sc/Fe-MIL-100 also exhibited the good catalytic performance for the tandem reactions [102].
In addition, Li et al. reported using bimetallic CuMo-BPY as catalyst for tandem process of olefin epoxidation plus \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) cycloaddition. When the tandem process was carried out in two separate steps by using TBHP as the oxidant, CuMo-BPY catalyzed the epoxidation of styrene towards styrene oxide with the yield of \(90.6\%\) at \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) reaction. And then, the produced styrene oxide was further reacted with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and cocatalyst tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBABr) to produce phenyl(ethylene carbonate) with the yield of \(90.0\%\) under the identical conditions. During the tandem process, the molybdenum oxide intermediate produced by the oxidation of TBHP acted as an electrophile to adsorb styrene and catalyze it to generate epoxy products. Then, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules were immobilized and activated by the alkaline \(\upmu{\mathrm{-OH}}\) tricopper (II) cores of CuMo-BPY, while the Br- anion conducted nucleophilic attack on the less hindered carbon atom in the epoxide to open the ring of the epoxide. Finally, the activated \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reacted with epoxides to generate final products through nucleophilic attack. Furthermore, CuMo-BPY also catalyzed the efficient conversion of other alkenes into cyclic carbonates, such as 4-chlorostyrene, cyclooctene, and cyclohexene [103].
In a word, bimetallic MOFs exhibit the unique physiochemical properties for achieving the high activity and excellent selectivity for tandem reactions, due to the synergistic effect of the two kinds of active metal sites in the framework.
## 3.2 Application of bimetallic MOF derivatives in thermal catalysis
Bimetallic MOFs derived functional nanomaterials such as carbometal composites, metal compounds, and metal oxide-carbon composites can be used as catalysts for different reactions. Here we discuss their catalytic applications for reduction and oxidation reactions.
## 3.2.1 Reduction reactions
Bimetallic MOF-derived carbon-metal composites can effectively promote the catalytic conversion of biomass derivatives. Jiang et al. calcined bimetallic Ni/M-MOF \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o},\) Mo, and La) with different ratios in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to obtain \(\mathrm{NiM}@C\) for the hydrogenation/hydrodeoxygenation of lignin derived phenolic compounds. During the hydrogenation of phenol, the conversion rate of phenol was \(66\%\) and the yield of cyclohexanol was \(50\%\) when monometallic \(\mathrm{Ni}@C\) was used as the catalyst. The introduction of Co, Mo, and La in \(\mathrm{NiM}@C\) significantly enhanced the catalytic performance. Among them, \(\mathrm{NiLa}_{0.33}@\mathrm{C}\) achieved complete conversion of phenol after \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , and the yield of cyclohexanol reached \(95\%\) (Figs. 9(a) and 9(b)). The high catalytic activity was attributed to the high Lewis acid sites and Bronsted acid sites of \(215.48\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) in total in \(\mathrm{NiLa}_{0.33}@\mathrm{C}\) with respect to other samples. These acidic sites not only promoted the cleavage of \(C{-}C\) bonds, but also cooperated with the hydrogen donor to generate a large amount of hydrogen gas, thereby promoting the hydrogenation reaction. Besides, \(\mathrm{NiLa}_{0.33}@\mathrm{C}\) also exhibited excellent catalytic performance for the hydrogenation/hydrodeoxygenation of guaiacol, eugenol, and lignin-derived dimers [104].
Similarly, Luo et al. prepared a series of highly dispersed CoNi alloy nanoparticles (NPs) embedded in porous nitrogen
Similarly, Luo et al. prepared a series of highly dispersed CoNi alloy nanoparticles (NPs) embedded in porous nitrogen containing carbon matrix multifunctional catalysts for achieving hydrogenation of furfural (FFA) to furfuryl alcohol (FAL). The effect of different chemical compositions on FFA hydrogenation was investigated by using Co@NC-800, \(x\mathrm{CoyNi@NC-800}\) , and \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{NC}{-}800\) at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and 2 MPa \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (Fig. 9(c)). Co@NC800 showed \(93.8\%\) selectivity of FAL and a lower FFA conversion rate of \(\sim72\%\) . In contrast, bimetallic \(x\mathrm{CoyNi@NC-800}\) catalysts showed a high FFA conversion rate of \(>99.9\%\) . Unfortunately, with further increase of Ni content, the significant decreased selectivity was obtained, such as \(92.7\%\) over \(2\mathrm{Co1Ni@NC-800}\) , \(\sim\) \(73\%\) over \(1\mathrm{Co1Ni@NC-800}\) , \(\sim63\%\) over \(1\mathrm{Co2Ni@NC-800}\) , and \(43\%\) over \(\mathrm{Ni@NC-800}\) . To investigate the influence of synergistic effect, the physical mixture of \(\mathrm{Co@NC-800}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni@NC-800}\) was tested under the identical conditions as comparison. The \(80.3\%\) conversion rate of FFA and \(76.3\%\) selectivity of FAL were obtained, indicating that the synergistic effect between Co and Ni played an important role in catalytic FFA hydrogenation to FAL. Further, Co acted as a Lewis acid site to absorb carbonyl and promote the activation of \(C{=}O\) bond, while Ni site changed the electronic structure of Co, thereby improving the catalytic performance. However, with the increase of Ni content in \(x\mathrm{CoyNi@NC-800}\) , FFA was mainly adsorbed on Ni in the form of \(\mathfrak{n}^{1}(\mathrm{O})\) -aldehyde and \(\mathfrak{n}^{2}(\mathrm{C},\ \mathrm{O})\) -aldehyde, so FFA tended to be completely hydrogenated totetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol (TFAL), leading to the gradually decreased selectivity of FAL [105].
Besides, Amal et al. applied NiMg-MOF-74 with different Ni/Mg ratios as the template to form a series of NiMg catalysts by thermal treatment at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . Different NiMg catalysts were used for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methane, and \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) showed the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion rate of \(62\%\) and methane selectivity higher than \(90\%\) at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 1 bar \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=1{:}4)\) (Figs. 9(d) and 9(e)). However, as the Ni/Mg ratio is greater than 4, the product selectivity shifts significantly towards CO regardless of the reaction temperature, so the methane selectivity over \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) and \(100\mathrm{Ni}\) are only about \(65\%\) and \(25\%\) under the identical conditions. These NiMg catalysts showed no negligible decrease of methane yield over \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) reaction during the stability test (Fig. 9(f)). The excellent catalytic performance of \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) was attributed to the following two aspects. On the one hand, the open \(\mathrm{Mg}\) sites promoted \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and facilitated the catalytic hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . On the other hand, highly dispersed Ni agglomerates acted as catalytic sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation. With insufficient \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , the intermediate \({}^{\ast}\mathrm{CO}\) was not further hydrogenated and was favorable for desorption due to the carbon shell-induced change in electronic state of Ni [95].
while inhibiting the formation of alkanes [106].
In a word, bimetallic MOF-derived functional materials provide an excellent platform for reduction reactions due to the multiple active sites and their synergistic effects.
## 3.2.2 Oxidation reactions
Bimetallic MOF-derived materials have been developed for catalytic oxidation reactions. Hu et al. prepared Cu-nanowire@Quasi-MOFs through thermal decomposition of benzimidazole-modified Cu/Co-MOFs for achieving the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under air atmosphere at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figs. 10(a)–10(c)). The pristine Cu/Co-MOFs exhibited only \(12.0\%\) conversion rate of benzyl alcohol with \(92.0\%\) selectivity of benzaldehyde. Cu/Co-MOFs calcined at \(260~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) gained \(90.7\%\) selectivity of benzaldehyde with \(32.5\%\) conversion rate of benzyl alcohol. Cu/Co-MOFs calcined at 280 and \(340~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) showed the lower activity of \(\sim\,50\%\) with the selectivity of benzaldehyde of \(\sim93\%\) and \(\sim52\%\) with the selectivity of benzaldehyde of \(\sim96\%\). In contrast, Cu-nanowire@Quasi-MOFs obtained by calcination of Cu/Co-MOFs at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) exhibited the increased performance of \(98.8\%\) selectivity of benzaldehyde and \(62.8\%\) conversion rate of benzyl alcohol. The catalytic activity of each calcined Cu/Co-MOFs was superior to that of Cu/Co-MOFs, because the active sites were easily exposed as the calcination temperature increased and solvent molecule escapes. The enhanced activity of Cu-nanowire@quasi-MOFs was attributed to the hierarchical porous structure of quasi-MOFs and high surface area of active Cu-nanowires for improving the mass transfer process and increasing the contact probability between the reactants and the catalyst [107].
Besides, metal oxides produced by pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs exhibit the good performance for catalytic oxidation reactions. Yeung et al. prepared a two-dimensional metal oxide \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) derived from the thermal decomposition of bimetallic CeCu-BDC as the catalyst for the oxidation of common volatile organic compounds (VOC). There are two main parameters for evaluating the catalytic performance for VOC oxidation, namely, the temperature required to achieve \(50\%\) (T50) and \(90\%\) (T90) conversion rate. When the prepared \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) was used as a
catalyst for the conversion of toluene to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), the T50 and T90 were 150 and \(186~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively (Fig. 10(d)). However, the comparative sample \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) required a temperature higher than \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to completely convert toluene into \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The comparative sample \(\mathrm{CuO}\) had a high light-off temperature, indicative of its poor reactivity. Correspondingly, the activation energy was \(52.4~\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\) over \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) with respect to \(57.5~\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\) over \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) and \(70.5~\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\) over \(\mathrm{CuO}\). The high activity of \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) originates from the formation of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}{-}\mathrm{O}_{x}{-}\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) via the substitution of cerium in the ceria lattice by copper and the lower formation energy of oxygen vacancies (i.e., \(2.27~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) (100)). In addition, \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) was also very active in catalyzing the oxidation of volatile organic pollutants, and the T50 is \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for methanol, \(87~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for acetone, and \(142~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for o-xylene (Fig. 10(e)). During the stability test for toluene oxidation at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(60~\mathrm{h}\) over \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\), the conversion rate decreased by less than \(1\%\). Such good stability was attributed to the molecular-level mixing of the two metal precursors for allowing \(\mathrm{CeCuO}_{x}\) in the form of single phase and the \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) lattice completely substituted by \(\mathrm{Cu}\) [108].
Similarly, Zaheer et al. calcined bimetallic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\)-MIL-88B in air to obtain \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanorods for the oxidation of benzyl alcohol. When Fe-MIL-88B and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\)-MIL-88B were used as catalysts, the conversion of benzyl alcohol was \(10\%\) and \(12\%\), respectively, and no target product benzaldehyde was produced. The conversions of \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanorods obtained by pyrolysis of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\)-MIL-88B at 600 and \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were \(15\%\) and \(20\%\), respectively, and the selectivity to benzaldehyde reached \(99\%\). When \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanorods obtained by pyrolysis at \(1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were used as the catalyst, the conversion of benzyl alcohol was \(86\%\) and the selectivity of benzaldehyde was \(77\%\). The high catalytic activity was mainly attributed to the mesoporous structure generated by \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) while maintaining a high specific surface area for effectively promoting the mass transfer process [109].
He et al. in-situ grew bimetallic \(\mathrm{Co/Zn-MOFs}\) on the surface of GO to obtain \(\mathrm{Co}_{2.5}\mathrm{Zn}_{97.5}\)-MOFs/GO-x, where \(x\) stands for the concentration of GO about 1, 2, and \(5~\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\). Then, the above composite material was heated to \(900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\) to obtain \(\mathrm{Co}_{2.5}\)-SAs@NC/GO-x. In the oxidation reaction of \(\mathrm{Hg}^{0}\) to \(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}\) in the gas, \(\mathrm{Co}_{2.5}\)-SAs@NC/GO-1 showed the best oxidation
efficiency (Fig. 10(f)). The effect of different Co contents on the oxidation efficiency was further studied, and the results showed that the highest oxidation efficiency was achieved when the loading amount of Co was \(0.075\;\mathrm{g}\), which was higher than that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2.5}\mathrm{SAs@NC-0.125\;g}\) (Fig. 10(g)). The high oxidation efficiency was attributed to the fact that a single Co atom can easily adsorb and activate gaseous oxygen to produce \(\mathrm{Co-O_{\beta}}\). At the same time, gaseous \(\mathrm{Hg^{0}}\) was adsorbed on the catalyst surface to form \(\mathrm{Hg^{0}(a d)}\), and finally \(\mathrm{Hg^{0}(a d)}\) reacts with \(\mathrm{Co-O_{\beta}}\) to form \(\mathrm{HgO}(\mathrm{ad})\) and then desorbed to obtain \(\mathrm{HgO}(\mathrm{g})\) (Fig. 10(h)) [110].
In a word, bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives show the excellent physiochemical properties due to the synergistic effect of the two kinds of metal sites in the framework. The two kinds of metal sites in bimetallic MOFs can work together or separately to modulate charge transfer or substrate adsorption and enhance the stability of the framework, resulting in superior performance that cannot be achieved by the individual metal sites.
## 4 Summary and outlook
Compared with monometallic MOFs, bimetallic MOFs have shown great advantages in the field of thermal catalysis due to their scalable active sites and significantly enhanced catalytic performances (Table 2). So far, different types of bimetallic MOFs have been successfully prepared by one-pot method and post
synthetic modification method. In addition, derived carbon-metal composites, metal compounds, and metal oxide-carbon composites can be obtained by pyrolyzing bimetallic MOFs and their composites. In this review, we summarize the recent advances in the preparation methods, characterization methods, and catalytic applications of bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives.
Although a lot of progress has been made in the synthesis and catalytic applications of bimetallic MOFs, it is still in its infancy, and there are still some problems and challenges: (1) For the synthesis of bimetallic MOFs, no matter what it is a one-pot method or post-synthetic modification method, it is difficult to precisely control final distribution of the two kinds of metal sites in the framework. In addition, trimetallic or multimetallic MOFs are rarely explored. (2) Some regular techniques such as TEM, XRD, FT-IR, and element mapping are generally applied for characterizing bimetallic MOFs, and more detailed characterization of the arrangement and location of metal sites in bimetallic MOFs are limited. (3) Despite the catalytic mechanisms of bimetallic MOF and its derivatives have been discussed in previous reports using various characterizations and theoretical calculations, clear understanding of the relationship among the structure, synergistic effects of bimetallic MOFs, and their catalytic performance is lacking. (4) The catalytic reaction system mainly focuses on the liquid phase reactions in batch reactors, and the
productivity is very limited. (5) Although bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives exhibited the excellent properties over monometallic MOFs due to their scalable active sites and synergistic interactions between the two kinds of metal sites, there is still a long distance from industrial application, especially in terms of synthesis cost and large-scale production process.
In view of the above problems, we put forward some suggestions for the future research on bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives: (1) Developing novel synthesis strategies for realizing precise synthesis of bimetallic MOFs at the atomic level and exploring new processes for achieving large-scale production under mild conditions. Besides, conducting research on trimetallic or multimetallic MOFs to integrate metal sites with different functions into the framework for multifunctional catalysis. (2) Developing the advanced techniques to analyze the crystal structures, especially for powder samples, as well as exploring in-situ characterization techniques to gain the detailed understanding of the formation process of bimetallic MOFs, such as in-situ XRD and advanced synchrotron radiation techniques, or different in-situ techniques coupled with each other. (3) Exploring the stability of bimetallic MOF and their derivatives by constructing different kinds of bimetallic nodes in the framework, such as iso-/heterovalent bimetallic MOFs using different synthesis methods. (4) The in-situ observation in the liquid phase is very limited for catalytic reactions, and thus exploring the potential in-situ techniques for liquid phase system is of great importance to monitor the reaction behavior during the reaction process, followed by using theoretical calculations to better understand the reaction mechanism. (5) Continuing to expand the liquid-phase catalytic reaction systems, especially in the synthesis of high value-added fine chemical products, such as the catalytic conversion of biomass, and developing continuous scale-up production processes.
In summary, bimetallic MOFs and their derivatives have shown the excellent performances in the field of thermal catalysis. We hope that this review will bring new insights to readers for better design and synthesis of bimetallic MOF-based catalysts and understanding of catalytic reaction mechanisms.
